      "text": "Let's color the hexagon at $(-5,8.7)$ yellow and the neighboring one at $(15,8.7)$ red, and we will deduce that this configuration is unique. Knowing this, we need to color the two remaining hexagons. We get:\n\n[asy]\npath a=(0,0)--(10,0)--(15,8.7)--(10,17.3)--(0,17.3)--(-5,8.7)--cycle;\ndraw(a);\ndraw(shift((15,8.7))*a);\ndraw(shift((15,-8.7))*a);\ndraw(shift((30,17.3))*a);\ndraw(shift((30,0))*a);\ndraw(shift((30,-17.3))*a);\ndraw(shift((45,26))*a);\ndraw(shift((45,8.7))*a);\ndraw(shift((45,-8.7))*a);\ndraw(shift((60,17.3))*a);\ndraw(shift((60,0))*a);\ndraw(shift((60,-17.3))*a);\ndraw(shift((75,8.7))*a);\ndraw(shift((75,-8.7))*a);\nlabel(\"$R$\",(-5,8.7));\ndraw((0,8.7)--(0,3)--(10,8.7));\n[/asy]\n\n$5$ distinct arrangements remain from adding a third yellow hexagon: we color the hexagon above, to the left of, below, to the right of, or next to the yellow hexagon to the top right. We can keep repeating this process, and notice that once we've colored the yellow hexagon next to the red one, say, to the left of it, we will be counting it twice. So we have a choice of either the hexagon to the right or above. This leaves $3$ colors and we get $3$ distinct ways of choosing which color to use, and $2$ ways of placing that color, plus the fact that we already counted the arrangement by swapping yellow for red.\n\nTherefore, there are $2(2+2)=\\boxed{8}$ ways to color the hexagon. The answer is 8",
