      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of different ways to color the hexagons with the restriction that no two hexagons sharing a common side are colored the same, we can use the concept of graph coloring. The problem can be visualized as a complete graph with 12 vertices (hexagons), where vertices are adjacent if they share a common side.\n\nFirst, note that a complete graph \\(K_n\\) with \\(n\\) vertices has \\((n-1)(n-2)(n-3)/6\\) edges. Here, \\(n = 12\\), so the total number of edges is:\n\\[\n\\frac{11 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 9}{6} = 165\n\\]\nWe need to color the vertices such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color. The chromatic polynomial for a cycle graph \\(C_n\\) (a hexagon here) is given by:\n\\[\nP(C_n, k) = (k-1)^n + (-1)^n (k-1)\n\\]\nwhere \\(k\\) is the number of colors. For our hexagon, \\(n = 6\\) and \\(k = 3\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\nP(C_6, 3) = (3-1)^6 + (-1)^6 (3-1) = 2^6 + 2 = 64 + 2 = 66\n\\]\nThe number 3 (number of colors) appears 3 times (once for the R color, twice for the two non-R colors), so the term \\((k-1)^n + (-1)^n (k-1)\\) will be multiplied by the same factor of 3. Thus, the coefficient of \\(x^6\\) in the expansion will be:\n\\[\n66 \\cdot 3 = 198\n\\]\nTherefore, the number of different ways to color the figure is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{198}\n\\]",
